  "issue_number": 191,
  "issue_title": "verify_benchmark.py crashes with FileNotFoundError instead of reporting 'missing meta.json'",
  "issue_body": "## Problem\n\n`scripts/verify_benchmark.py::verify_dir` is explicitly designed to report a missing manifest as a clean problem:\n\n```python\n# verify_dir(), line 91-92\nif not meta_path.exists():\n    return [f\"missing {meta_path.name}\"]\n```\n\nBut `main()` re-reads `meta.json` **unconditionally**, right after calling `verify_dir` and **before** the `if problems:` report block:\n\n```python\n# main(), line 145-146\nproblems = verify_dir(args.dir, args.password)\nmeta = json.loads((Path(args.dir) / \"meta.json\").read_text())   # <-- crashes if meta.json is missing\n```\n\nSo when a build is missing `meta.json` (a realistic partial/corrupted build \u2014 the single most basic integrity failure), the verifier dies with an uncaught `FileNotFoundError` traceback instead of printing the intended `FAIL [full: ...] \u2014 1 problem(s): - missing meta.json` and exiting 1. The whole job of the verifier is to *report* integrity problems, and it crashes on the most basic one.\n\n`meta` is only consumed in the success branch (the `OK [...]` line and the optional `--append`), so reading it up front on the failure path is both unnecessary and wrong.\n\n## Reproduce (offline, no password/decryption reached)\n\n```\nBENCHMARK_PASSWORD=x python scripts/verify_benchmark.py --dir <dir_without_meta.json>\n```\n`verify_dir` returns early at the missing-meta check (no decryption), then line 146 raises `FileNotFoundError`.\n\n## Fix\n\nDefer the `meta.json` read into the OK branch (after the `if problems:` block), tracking only the meta path in each mode. When execution reaches the read, verification has passed, so the file exists and is well-formed.",
  "merged_at": "2026-07-11T08:58:48.000Z",
